const systemsSyllabi = {
  'Technical Fundamentals': r'''
Understand how software communicates, persists data, ships changes and recovers from failures. | Trace requests through system boundaries. | Test and debug software with version control. | Apply secure, observable delivery practices. | Basic terminal navigation. | Python 3, Git, SQLite | Deliver a local service with route contracts, tests, scoped data access and an incident runbook.
# HTTP and resources | Describe requests, responses and client behavior. | Document success, invalid-input and missing-resource cases for one route.
Request structure | A method states the operation, a path identifies a resource and query parameters narrow it. Credentials in URLs risk exposure through histories and logs. | Method; Path; Query | from urllib.parse import urlsplit\nu=urlsplit('https://example.com/orders?limit=5')\nprint(u.path,u.query) | /orders limit=5 | API designers publish predictable filtered lists. | Parse two parameters and explain where credentials should be handled.
Status families | Typical HTTP status families distinguish success, client-side problems and server failures. Retry behavior should depend on both status and operation safety. | 2xx; 4xx; 5xx | 200 OK; 404 missing resource; 503 temporarily unavailable | Success, client error, server error. | SDKs avoid endlessly retrying invalid requests. | Contrast handling of 400 and 503.
? Where should persistent credentials generally stay out of? | URL query strings | Secure identity handling | Protected credential stores | URLs can be copied into logs and histories.
? Which status represents a missing requested resource? | 404 | 200 | 503 | 404 indicates absence of the resource.
# Processes and files | Make runtime inputs and failures explicit. | Write a script that validates paths and returns meaningful status.
Path resolution | Relative paths start at the process working directory, which may differ from the script location. Resolve and validate paths rather than assuming the launcher directory. | cwd; Relative path; Validation | from pathlib import PurePosixPath\nprint(PurePosixPath('/work/app')/'data.csv') | /work/app/data.csv | Scheduled jobs avoid reading the wrong extract. | Run a script from its own directory and from its parent.
Exit codes | Zero conventionally means success; nonzero indicates failure or a specific non-success outcome. Automation should preserve exit codes and expose useful diagnostics. | Exit code; stderr; Subprocess | import subprocess,sys\np=subprocess.run([sys.executable,'-c','raise SystemExit(3)'])\nprint(p.returncode) | 3 | CI halts after a failed check. | Use check=True and handle the resulting exception.
? What is the default base for a relative path? | Process working directory | Always the script folder | DNS server | Relative filesystem operations use the current working directory.
? What does exit status 3 ordinarily communicate? | Non-success | Guaranteed success | Bytes written | Nonzero codes communicate a failed or special outcome.
# Git collaboration | Stage intentional changes and test integrations. | Create a local branch and review a narrow commit.
Staging snapshots | The index holds the next commit's snapshot. Editing a staged file adds unstaged changes until it is staged again. | Working tree; Index; Commit | In a local Git repository: git add README.md\ngit diff --cached -- README.md | The staged README patch. | Reviewers inspect scoped changes before merging. | Stage one file while keeping a second file unstaged.
Branches and merging | A branch points at a commit and advances with new commits. Conflict resolution needs behavioral review and tests, not merely removal of conflict markers. | Branch; Merge; Conflict | git switch -c report-fix\ngit status --short --branch | Branch report-fix in a local repository. | Teams develop independently and integrate reviewed work. | Resolve two local edits to the same sentence and test the merged result.
? Which command shows the staged patch? | git diff --cached | git log alone | git status alone | It compares the index with the current commit.
? What should follow manual conflict resolution? | Testing integrated behavior | Ignoring the result | Deleting all other work | A resolved file can still contain incorrect combined logic.
# Tests and debugging | Reproduce bugs and identify boundary failures. | Submit a failing test, minimal reproduction and corrected behavior.
Threshold tests | Test adjacent values around a branch boundary and invalid input under a documented contract. Large ordinary values alone rarely reveal off-by-one defects. | Unit test; Boundary; Contract | def fee(n):return 0 if n>=100 else 5\nassert (fee(99),fee(100))==(5,0) | Assertions pass. | Billing systems protect pricing thresholds. | Add negative and nonnumeric cases with explicit expected behavior.
Structured logs | Record event, stage and correlation ID without unnecessary private payloads. Consistent fields make automated queries and cross-service tracing easier. | Severity; Correlation; Redaction | import json\nprint(json.dumps({'event':'job_failed','job_id':'r42','reason':'timeout'})) | A JSON event with ID and reason. | Operators connect failures across pipeline stages. | Add duration and stage without logging credentials.
? Which values test a threshold at 100 directly? | 99 and 100 | Only 500 | Only an empty table | Adjacent values probe the branch boundary.
? What connects logs for one request? | Correlation ID | Terminal width | A screenshot | Shared identifiers relate events across stages.
# APIs and durability | Make retries safe and writes consistent. | Design an order operation with a durable request key.
Idempotency | Repeating an equivalent request should preserve its intended effect. Deduplication must be durable and atomic with the effect for reliable recovery. | Request key; Retry; Atomicity | r={};r.setdefault('req42','order1');r.setdefault('req42','order2')\nprint(r) | {'req42': 'order1'} in this local illustration. | Commerce prevents duplicate orders after response loss. | Explain why a production process needs a database constraint rather than only this dictionary.
Constraints | A database uniqueness constraint arbitrates concurrent inserts. A pre-check alone can race when two processes both observe absence. | Unique key; Race; Transaction | CREATE TABLE booking(ref TEXT UNIQUE);\nINSERT INTO booking VALUES('b1');\nINSERT INTO booking VALUES('b1'); | Second insert fails in SQLite. | Reservations enforce exclusive booking references. | Wrap two related writes in a transaction and test rollback.
? What stays the same under idempotent retries? | Intended state effect | Exact response latency | Every log timestamp | Repeating the operation must not duplicate its effect.
? What should enforce uniqueness under concurrent writes? | A durable database constraint | A frontend label | A log formatter | Database constraints coordinate competing writes.
# Runtime reliability | Bound waiting and avoid retry amplification. | Allocate a total deadline and bounded retry schedule.
Timeout budgets | The total deadline includes call duration, setup and backoff. Per-attempt timeouts cannot independently exceed the user's remaining budget. | Timeout; Deadline; Backoff | Budget 1000 ms; two 350 ms calls plus 100 ms wait | 800 ms before other overhead. | APIs release resources when dependencies stall. | Check three 250 ms attempts plus two 150 ms waits against the budget.
Retry jitter | Retry transient and repeat-safe operations with a bounded count. Jitter spreads client retries so recovery is not overwhelmed by synchronized bursts. | Transient failure; Jitter; Limit | import random\nprint(round(random.Random(3).uniform(0,.2),3)) | .048 seconds | Client fleets smooth retry traffic after outages. | Define a maximum schedule and exclude validation errors.
? What must fit in a request deadline? | All calls and waits | Only DNS | Only the last attempt | Users experience the full cumulative wait.
? Why randomize retry delays? | To spread synchronized bursts | To guarantee recovery | To increase payload size | Jitter reduces simultaneous retries.
# Access and inputs | Enforce account ownership and data contracts. | Review a service for cross-account reads and unsafe query construction.
Authorization | Authentication establishes identity; authorization checks what that identity can do to a particular resource. A valid login alone does not prove ownership. | Identity; Ownership; Permission | owner='acct-a';acting='acct-b'\nprint(owner==acting) | False | Account-based services isolate records. | Add a scoped read-only auditor rule and test ordinary users remain isolated.
Bound parameters | Validate inputs at boundaries and bind them as database values. Keep SQL command syntax separate from user text and use least-privilege connections. | Parameter; Type; Length limit | import sqlite3\nc=sqlite3.connect(':memory:')\nprint(c.execute('SELECT ?',('sample',)).fetchone()) | ('sample',) | Search APIs handle arbitrary user text safely. | Add a maximum length and missing-value response.
? Which check controls access to one user's record? | Authorization | DNS | Authentication alone | Permission must be checked against the specific resource.
? How should search text enter a SQL query? | Bound parameter | String concatenation into syntax | As a logged credential | Parameters preserve the syntax-data boundary.
# Delivery and observability | Release gradually and diagnose the changed component. | Publish a release checklist, rollback criterion and on-call runbook.
Canary checks | Compare a small new-version traffic share against the baseline under the same measures. Pause when agreed failure or latency guardrails are breached. | Canary; Rollback; Guardrail | Baseline errors 1%; canary 4%; limit 2%. | Pause and investigate before expanding exposure. | Teams limit the impact of a faulty build. | Specify minimum sample size and representative traffic slices.
Service signals | Review traffic, errors, duration and saturation together. Tail latency can deteriorate for a minority even when the mean appears stable. | Latency; Percentile; Saturation | Mean remains 100 ms but p95 rises from 200 to 900 ms. | Tail behavior needs investigation. | Operators find an upstream affecting only some routes. | Design a route-level dashboard and escalation threshold.
? Why start a rollout with limited traffic? | Contain impact while verifying | Avoid measuring failures | Expose everyone immediately | A staged release bounds the blast radius.
? Which measure reveals slow responses hidden in the mean? | Upper latency percentile | Logo size | Deployment label | Upper percentiles describe tail delays.
''',
  'Artificial Intelligence': r'''
Study AI agents, search, inference and decisions with transparent examples and assurance practices. | Represent states, actions and constraints. | Implement search and decision policies. | Evaluate failures and design human oversight. | Basic Python and probability. | Python 3, collections, heapq | Build a dispatch planner with search heuristics, hard constraints, error analysis and a human fallback.
# Agents and objectives | Translate business goals into observable actions and limits. | Specify observations, actions, costs and fallback for inventory control.
Agent loop | An agent observes state, selects an action and sees the consequence. Distinguish missing information from a confirmed zero because they justify different actions. | State; Action; Observation | stock=0\nprint('reorder' if stock<2 else 'wait') | reorder | Inventory systems react to measured stock. | Add a manual-review action for unknown stock.
Constraints and proxies | Hard constraints restrict allowed actions; a soft objective ranks the feasible ones. Check whether optimizing a proxy creates unwanted behavior. | Objective; Proxy; Feasibility | Route A: 8 min but overweight; route B: 12 min within capacity. | B is feasible under the weight rule. | Dispatch respects safety before travel time. | Write an objective balancing delay and missed deliveries.
? Why distinguish unknown stock from zero? | Missingness and confirmed absence imply different decisions | Both always mean reorder | Unknown is a numeric type | Uncertainty needs explicit policy.
? Which route is admissible under a hard capacity rule? | The within-capacity route | Any faster route | Both regardless of load | Constraints define the feasible set.
# Uninformed search | Traverse graphs with termination and path reconstruction. | Solve an unweighted grid using BFS and compare DFS behavior.
Breadth-first search | BFS uses FIFO order to visit states by edge count. It yields shortest hop paths on unweighted graphs when state identity and visited tracking are correct. | FIFO; BFS; Visited | from collections import deque\nq=deque(['start']);q.extend(['left','right'])\nprint([q.popleft() for _ in range(3)]) | ['start','left','right'] | Dependency tools locate minimum-hop paths. | Record parent pointers and reconstruct a path.
Cycle handling | A route is not a new state if it reaches an already-seen configuration. Mark visited on enqueue in ordinary BFS to avoid redundant frontier work. | State key; Cycle; Deduplication | g={'A':['B'],'B':['A']};seen={'A','B'}\nprint([n for n in g['B'] if n not in seen]) | [] | Graph walkers terminate despite cyclic references. | Add a third node and test reachability.
? What order does BFS use? | FIFO | LIFO | Descending labels only | A queue processes the earliest frontier states first.
? What prevents infinite A→B→A exploration? | Visited state IDs | Removing the goal | Always choosing first edge | State deduplication cuts cycles.
# Heuristics and feasibility | Prioritize promising paths without weakening constraints. | Compare a zero heuristic and grid-distance heuristic.
A-star estimates | A* prioritizes cost spent plus estimated remaining cost. A lower-bound admissible heuristic supports optimality under the standard assumptions. | g; h; Admissibility | g=5;x,y=1,2;gx,gy=4,6\nprint(g+abs(gx-x)+abs(gy-y)) | 12 | Warehouse robots prioritize plausible routes. | Explain why diagonal movement can invalidate a Manhattan lower bound.
Feasible selection | Filter hard violations before choosing the least soft cost. If none remain, report infeasibility instead of silently returning an invalid action. | Feasibility; Cost; Empty set | a=[('A',5,False),('B',7,True),('C',8,True)]\nprint(min((c,n) for n,c,ok in a if ok)) | (7,'B') | Workforce planners honor work-hour rules. | Handle an empty feasible list with an explicit review result.
? What expression does A* prioritize? | g+h | h/g | Visited count | It combines incurred and estimated remaining costs.
? What should happen when every action violates a hard rule? | Report infeasible for review | Pick the cheapest invalid one silently | Invent a valid one | No authorized candidate exists.
# Rules and inference | Derive conclusions and update beliefs with evidence. | Build eligibility rules that record their supporting facts.
Forward chaining | Apply rules whose premises are known, add conclusions and repeat until no new fact is produced. Provenance makes the resulting decision explainable. | Premise; Fact; Fixed point | f={'paid','verified'}\nif {'paid','verified'}<=f:f.add('approved')\nprint('approved' in f) | True | Business workflows apply auditable policies. | Add an exception and define precedence.
Bayesian evidence | If evidence is equally likely under both conditions, its likelihood ratio is one and the prior does not change. Base rates still matter. | Prior; Likelihood; Posterior | p=.2;l=.4\nprint(round(p*l/(p*l+(1-p)*l),2)) | .2 | Incident diagnosis weighs the informativeness of signals. | Double likelihood under an incident and recompute.
? When does forward chaining reach a fixed point? | No rule adds a new fact | After the first input | Only after a timer | All currently derivable conclusions have been added.
? Equally likely evidence under both hypotheses does what? | Leaves prior unchanged | Forces probability one | Forces .5 | The likelihood ratio is one.
# Decisions and planning | Evaluate uncertain outcomes over multiple steps. | Compare maintenance choices with expected and worst-case costs.
Expected utility | Sum outcome utilities weighted by their probabilities. An attractive average does not remove hard safety limits or severe downside concerns. | Utility; Probability; Risk | print(.8*50+.2*(-100)) | 20 | Maintenance planning weighs benefits and downtime. | Find the break-even success probability.
Discounted return | A policy maps states to actions; discounting reduces the contribution of distant rewards. Reward order matters when the discount factor is below one. | Policy; Gamma; Return | r=[5,10];g=.9\nprint(sum(g**i*v for i,v in enumerate(r))) | 14 | Scheduling balances immediate work with future backlog. | Reverse rewards and compare the returns.
? Expected utility for 80% of 50 and 20% of -100 is what? | 20 | 40 | -50 | Weighted terms 40 and -20 sum to 20.
? What defines a policy? | Action choice for each considered state | One number without state | A list of labels | Policies map observations or states to decisions.
# Learning signals | Distinguish labeled prediction and reward-driven control. | Choose a learning setup for ticket topic and escalation strategy.
Labels versus rewards | Supervised learning uses target labels. Reinforcement learning associates rewards with actions and can face delayed consequences and changing states. | Label; Reward; Delay | Topic examples with category labels → classifier; resolution reward after escalation → sequential policy. | Different feedback and evaluation designs. | Support tools classify before deciding an action. | Explain how click-only rewards could encourage distracting suggestions.
Bounded exploration | Exploration gathers information but may impose real cost. Start in simulation or offline analysis and restrict pilot actions to acceptable risk. | Explore; Exploit; Sandbox | Compare candidate schedules in simulation before bounded production exposure. | Evidence is gathered without unrestricted live experimentation. | Industrial planners avoid testing dangerous actions on real equipment. | Define measurements needed to enlarge the pilot.
? What is the usual training signal for supervised classification? | Labeled examples | Only delayed rewards | A search heuristic alone | Targets label the expected output.
? Why limit production exploration? | New actions can incur real harm or cost | Exploration guarantees optimality | Simulation exactly matches reality | Operational uncertainty requires bounds.
# Responsible evaluation | Measure coverage and concentrated error. | Review an abstention policy on language and workload slices.
Abstention metrics | A deferring system must report both handled-case accuracy and coverage. Otherwise it could appear perfect by refusing almost everything. | Abstain; Coverage; Accuracy | print(80/100,76/80) | .8 coverage and .95 handled accuracy | Claims intake routes uncertain cases to reviewers. | Compare 50 handled and 49 correct under a stricter threshold.
Slice checks | Disaggregate failures by relevant population and scenario. Small samples need further evidence rather than assumptions about causes. | Slice; Support; Representation | A:90/100 correct; B:4/10; aggregate:94/110 | The aggregate hides much weaker performance on B. | Multilingual support checks each supported language. | Design additional representative B sampling.
? Which measure accompanies accuracy when cases may be deferred? | Coverage | File size | Screen count | Coverage reveals how often the system handles cases.
? What should poor subgroup results prompt? | Slice-level investigation | Hide the slice | Declare equal quality | Aggregate performance misses localized errors.
# Assurance and operations | Preserve human authority and stage releases. | Submit an override flow, audit schema and rollback rule.
Human review | A reviewer needs adequate context, authority to override and an escalation path. Minimize private data while recording the evidence and policy version behind decisions. | Oversight; Override; Provenance | Record rule version, relevant signals, approved action and reviewer reason. | A bounded auditable intervention. | High-impact recommendations remain reviewable. | Specify the minimum data required to audit an override.
Shadow deployment | Log candidate decisions on live inputs while the baseline retains control. Compare outcomes before authorizing broader action and define rollback criteria. | Shadow; Baseline; Rollback | Candidate plans are recorded; current scheduler still dispatches. | Side-by-side evidence without changed actions. | Operations evaluates new policies safely. | Define a release threshold using missed-service commitments.
? What makes oversight meaningful? | Context and genuine override authority | A reviewer name with no controls | Unusable escalation | A person needs power and information to intervene.
? What happens during shadow operation? | Candidate outputs are recorded without driving live actions | Candidate replaces production immediately | Baseline stops | Shadow mode produces evidence under existing control.
''',
